.TH "SPGEN-TEST_SUPPORT" "1" "" "spgen 0.1.0" ""
.SH NAME
spgen\-test_support \- Generates a read model's test\-support fixtures and factory functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
mix sp.gen.test_support [options] <READ\-MODEL>
.SH DESCRIPTION
The model's fixtures — declared data for tests — and the factories that turn fixtures into persisted read models. Two outputs, two ownership models: the fixture file is a projection of the model (rewritten whole), the factory file is the composite's append\-only ledger (one new_* pair per read model).
.SH Prerequisite
.IP \[bu] 2
The Domain Model (../../../../domain\-model/README.md) read model carries a schema block; fixtures are its schema.examples.
.IP \[bu] 2
Each example names itself with a fixture key as its id: <composite>:<read\-model>:<mnemonic>, kebab\-case segments (naming (../../naming\-substitution.md#fixture\-keys)).
.IP \[bu] 2
Example fields must be declared by the schema and carry its required properties; values in *Id fields are fixture keys or real UUIDs.
.IP \[bu] 2
mix sp.check is the gate: it reports every violation of this contract before and independently of generation.
.SH "What the generator does"
Fixtures file (test/support/<_subdomain>/<composite>/fixtures/<domain_object>s.ex): one zero\-arity function per example, in model order — mnemonic snake\-cased (equipment:apparatus:parallel\-bars → parallel_bars/0), returning the read model's struct, typed with the read model's own @spec. Ids and references materialize as UUID.uuid5(:dns, "<key>"): one mnemonic names one thing in every environment forever, and every value is a real UUID. The aggregate function (def apparatuses, do: [...]) lists them all. The @moduledoc carries provenance: the file is owned by the model.
.PP
Factory file (test/support/<_subdomain>/<composite>/<composite>.ex): new_<domain_object>/1 with two clauses — the fixture struct (mapped to attributes; Ecto's cast/3 takes a :map, not a struct) and a plain attribute map — and new_<domain_object>s/1, which raises with the changeset errors when a seed fails: a half\-seeded world is the most confusing failure mode a test can have. Aliases for the read model and the subdomain Repo land with their first consumer.
.PP
A read model without examples still gets its factory: the factory is about the schema's shape, the fixtures are about its data. A run against a model whose examples violate the fixture contract fails with every message and writes nothing.
.PP
Two rules keep this generator inside its own bounds. The read model is a dependency of both blueprints: it is the one foreign citizen the run may create, and it arrives in\-run so ReadModel.t() and the struct always resolve. And its closure scope is :none: creating its own files never triggers the neighborhood sweep — units a human deleted in events, queries or commands stay deleted. Generators whose code is referenced by others keep the :all sweep. A test scaffold that seeds through the factory does reference these files, but its blueprint owns the whole file and creates it in one shot (gates only defer a merge into a file that already exists, by which time the fixtures are there), so :none stands: flipping to :all would resurrect seeds a human removed.
.SH "Left to you"
.IP \[bu] 2
The examples themselves are domain content: declare them in the read model.
.IP \[bu] 2
Hand\-written seeds: the struct clause's absence reports as Outdated with the exact clause to add — the merge never edits an existing function.
.IP \[bu] 2
Naming drift is a refactoring: when an example is renamed, fixtures regenerate and stale call sites fail compilation — update the callers' references; never re\-run the test\-producing generators to "repair" them.
.SH Tests
No test file of its own: the fixtures are tested by their consumers, and SpGen covers the generator itself (fixtures, projection semantics, the sp.check engine) in tools/spgen/test.
.SS "Generated files"
.IP \[bu] 2
services/api/test/support/<_subdomain>/<composite>/fixtures/<domain_object>s.ex (fixture functions per example)
.IP \[bu] 2
services/api/test/support/<_subdomain>/<composite>/<composite>.ex (factory functions per example)
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-subdomain\fR \fI<SUBDOMAIN>\fR

.TP
\fB\-\-composite\fR \fI<COMPOSITE>\fR

.TP
\fB\-\-constituent\fR \fI<CONSTITUENT>\fR

.TP
\fB\-\-model\fR \fI<MODEL>\fR
model root, default docs/domain\-model (relative to \-\-root)
.TP
\fB\-\-root\fR \fI<ROOT>\fR
repo root, default inferred from the current directory
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
report the drift, write nothing
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-force\fR
FE: rewrite present generated units in place
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fIREAD\-MODEL\fR>
the element: esdm: reference, *.esdm.yaml, kind.name, or bare name
.SH "SEE ALSO"
